const lessonSlides = {"lessons":[{"id":"u1-w01-l01","lesson":1,"title":"Plan a badge-cost program","prepare":"Whiteboard or paper; chapter open. No Python installation needed for this lesson.","slides":[{"title":"Plan a badge-cost program","body":["Unit 1 · Week 1 · Lesson 1","Explain the problem before writing code."],"minutes":2,"notes":"Introduce the week: plan, run, trace/test, then apply. Ask learners to explain what a successful badge calculator would do. This lesson is diagnostic and formative.","strategy":"Learning intention"},{"title":"What does a program need?","body":["A club sells badges for $3 each.","Think alone: what input do we need?","Compare with a partner: what should four badges cost?","Share your method, then try six badges."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Allow 45 seconds of silent thinking, one minute comparing, then invite two explanations. Expected: quantity is input, 4 × 3 = $12, 6 × 3 = $18. Accept diagrams or spoken explanations. If learners add 3 to the quantity, use equal groups to model the price per badge.","strategy":"Think, pair, share"},{"title":"Today’s goal","body":["I can identify input, processing and output.","I can write calculation steps in order.","I can predict a result before testing."],"minutes":6,"notes":"Have learners restate one criterion in their own words. Distinguish the variable quantity from the stated fixed price. Return to these criteria at the exit check.","strategy":"Success criteria"},{"title":"The badge requirement","body":["Display the cost for a valid badge quantity.","Each badge costs $3.","Assume a whole-number quantity of zero or more.","Input validation is taught later."],"minutes":10,"notes":"Think aloud as you underline quantity, circle the price and identify the required output. Model why zero is allowed by the stated requirement. Ask everyone to identify one valid input before continuing. If several learners choose a negative quantity, revisit the assumption. Validation comes later.","strategy":"Teacher modelling"},{"title":"A worked example: four badges","table":[["Stage","Badge example"],["Input","Quantity: 4"],["Processing","4 × 3 = 12"],["Output","Total: $12"]],"minutes":10,"notes":"Model input 4, processing 4 × 3 and output Total: $12, explaining each decision. Then learners create the same three rows for quantity 6 without copying the numbers. Ask everyone to show their processing row. Expected $18. If they write 6 + 3, model equal groups again before independent practice.","strategy":"Worked example and guided practice"},{"title":"An algorithm for any quantity","body":["Write the steps without looking at the example.","Trace your steps for 0, 1 and 6 badges.","Explain your sequence to a partner.","Revise any step your partner cannot follow."],"minutes":12,"notes":"Allow six minutes independently, three minutes of explanation in pairs, then three minutes to revise. Expected sequence: read quantity, multiply by 3, display total. Expected totals $0, $3, $18. Support: offer the three stage names Input, Processing, Output. Stretch: explain why displaying total before assigning it fails. Keep individual plans as evidence.","strategy":"Independent practice and peer explanation"},{"title":"Which rule matches the requirement?","body":["For six badges, choose a rule on paper.","A: quantity + 3     B: quantity × 3","Show your choice together.","Explain what the other rule gets wrong."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Collect a simultaneous response before discussion so every learner commits. Correct B gives $18. A gives $9 and adds a price once rather than charging for each badge. If several choose A, use a drawing of six groups of $3 and ask a fresh check for two badges before moving on.","strategy":"All-student misconception check"},{"title":"Exit check: seven badges","body":["Name the input.","State the processing step.","Give the expected displayed total.","Explain why an expected result is useful."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Collect an individual written response without partner help. Expected input quantity 7, processing multiply by 3, Total: $21. An independent expectation checks correctness. Sort responses into ready, input/processing confusion and calculation support, and use these groups to plan the next lesson starter.","strategy":"Independent exit ticket"}]},{"id":"u1-w01-l02","lesson":2,"title":"Run your first Python script","prepare":"School-approved Python 3 editor/IDE, writable practice folder and projector. Check the school’s Run command before class.","slides":[{"title":"Run your first Python script","body":["Unit 1 · Week 1 · Lesson 2","Turn the badge algorithm into a saved program."],"minutes":2,"notes":"Use the school-approved environment. Do not ask learners to install software or change school settings during the lesson.","strategy":"Learning intention"},{"title":"Retrieve the badge plan","body":["Close your notes for one minute.","Name the input and calculation step.","Predict the cost of six badges.","Compare with your notes and correct one detail."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Use one minute of individual retrieval, then compare and correct. Expected quantity, quantity × 3, $18. Ask everyone to show the calculation. If confusion remains between input and processing, briefly return to the three-row badge example before opening the editor.","strategy":"Retrieval practice"},{"title":"Today’s goal","body":["Save and run a Python script.","Enter suitable data when prompted.","Compare actual output with a prediction."],"minutes":6,"notes":"Success: the learner saves badge_cost.py, runs it independently, identifies where to enter the quantity and records a matching prediction/result.","strategy":"Success criteria"},{"title":"From demonstration to your own file","body":["Watch the teacher save badge_cost.py.","Point out the editor and output area.","Create your own file in this week’s folder.","Show your partner where Save and Run are."],"minutes":10,"notes":"Demonstrate in the school-approved editor for three minutes, then pause for learners to repeat each step. Ask partners to point to controls on their own screens, without taking over. Check everyone has a saved .py file before continuing. Save stores changes and Run executes them. Provide a prepared folder or printed instructions for setup support. Avoid filenames that shadow modules, such as random.py.","strategy":"Demonstration with guided rehearsal"},{"title":"The badge script","code":"BADGE_PRICE = 3\nquantity = int(input(\"Number of badges: \"))\ntotal = quantity * BADGE_PRICE\nprint(f\"Total: ${total}\")","minutes":10,"notes":"Read the code top to bottom and connect each line to the paper plan. Ask learners to point to the line that reads input before you explain it. input returns text and int converts suitable whole-number text. Pause before the total line and elicit a prediction for quantity 4. print displays the result. Uppercase BADGE_PRICE signals an intended fixed value, but Python does not enforce it. Model the reasoning rather than only typing.","strategy":"Code reading and teacher think-aloud"},{"title":"A prediction before each run","body":["Write your prediction for four badges.","Run the saved script and enter 4.","Compare expected and actual output with a partner.","Repeat for 1 and 0, predicting each time."],"minutes":12,"notes":"Use individual predictions before learners work in pairs. Driver runs the file and navigator checks the prediction and explains a line, then swap for the next quantity. Expected $12, $3, $0. If a result differs, inspect the saved file and input before changing code. Each learner retains a prediction/result record. Use suitable inputs only.","strategy":"Predict, run, investigate"},{"title":"One change, a new prediction","body":["Change BADGE_PRICE from 3 to 5.","Predict four badges before running.","Explain why the input line stays the same.","Return the price to 3 after checking."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Expected $20. Ask all learners to write why quantity still needs input while the fixed price changes. If the output stays $12, check saving and which file is running. Support: highlight the price line. Stretch: predict a new price and quantity before testing. Keep the original file structure.","strategy":"Controlled variation and explanation"},{"title":"Exit check: run independently","body":["Show your saved Python file.","Run it for two badges at $3 each.","State the expected output before running.","Explain the difference between Save and Run."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Each learner saves and runs their own file for quantity 2 at price 3, predicting Total: $6 first. Ask for a spoken or written explanation of Save versus Run. Record setup difficulties separately from conceptual errors. Start the next lesson with a short demonstration for learners who need environment support.","strategy":"Independent performance check"}]},{"id":"u1-w01-l03","lesson":3,"title":"Trace, modify and test a sequence","prepare":"Working badge_cost.py at price 3; paper trace table or worksheet; Python editor.","slides":[{"title":"Trace, modify and test","body":["Unit 1 · Week 1 · Lesson 3","Explain what changes as each instruction runs."],"minutes":2,"notes":"Learners should already be able to run the badge script. Keep a clean copy available so setup errors do not consume the tracing lesson.","strategy":"Learning intention"},{"title":"Retrieve the script","code":"BADGE_PRICE = 3\nquantity = int(input(\"Number of badges: \"))\ntotal = quantity * BADGE_PRICE\nprint(f\"Total: ${total}\")","body":["Predict the output for quantity 5."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Learners predict Total: $15 individually before running. Ask them to annotate which line reads input, which calculates and which displays. Compare annotations in pairs. If they treat print as the calculation, trace the total assignment explicitly before moving on.","strategy":"Retrieval and code annotation"},{"title":"Today’s goal","body":["Trace variable values in execution order.","Modify a calculation to match a requirement.","Record expected and actual results."],"minutes":6,"notes":"Success: complete a trace for a new input, add a fixed charge correctly, and explain at least one meaningful test. A trace records state after each instruction, not just the final answer.","strategy":"Success criteria"},{"title":"Trace four badges","table":[["After instruction","Value known"],["BADGE_PRICE = 3","BADGE_PRICE is 3"],["Read quantity 4","quantity is 4"],["Calculate total","total is 12"],["Display total","Output: Total: $12"]],"minutes":10,"notes":"Think aloud through the model for quantity 4. Cover the model or move off this slide and ask learners to trace quantity 2 using blank rows. Expected price 3, quantity 2, total 6 and output Total: $6. total has no assigned value before its calculation. Support: retain instruction labels. Stretch: explain which values remain unchanged at each line. Check every learner’s total row before the next task.","strategy":"Worked trace and faded support"},{"title":"Add a fixed packaging charge","code":"PACKAGING = 2\ntotal = quantity * BADGE_PRICE + PACKAGING","body":["The $2 charge applies to every order, including zero badges.","Predict the totals for quantities 0 and 3."],"minutes":10,"notes":"Model adding PACKAGING = 2 above the total calculation. Learners explain in pairs why packaging is added once, then independently predict quantity 0 ($2) and 3 ($11). Check answers before implementation. Contrast the stated zero-order rule with possible alternative requirements without changing this task. Multiplication precedes addition here.","strategy":"Guided prediction and code modification"},{"title":"Test the modified program","table":[["Quantity","Expected","Actual"],["0","$2","Record your run"],["1","$5","Record your run"],["3","$11","Record your run"]],"minutes":12,"notes":"Driver implements and runs the modification. Navigator checks the requirement and compares expected with actual output. Swap roles after the first case. Every learner keeps their own test record. Expected $2, $5, $11. Keep expected values unchanged when actual differs. Support: provide the correct input and output lines. Stretch: find a quantity where an incorrect packaging-per-item calculation accidentally passes (quantity 1).","strategy":"Paired testing with role changes"},{"title":"Two rules, different costs","body":["For quantity 3, which rule charges packaging once?","A: quantity × 3 + 2     B: quantity × (3 + 2)","Choose alone, then explain to a partner.","Why does a test with quantity 1 miss the error?"],"minutes":5,"notes":"Take simultaneous A/B responses before partner discussion. A gives $11 and B gives $15. Both are syntactically valid, so B is a logic error for this requirement. Quantity 1 produces $5 for both and cannot distinguish them. If several choose B, model one packaging charge separately and recheck quantity 2: $8 versus $10. A syntax error prevents Python parsing the instructions.","strategy":"Hinge question and error analysis"},{"title":"Exit check: a changed order","body":["Badges cost $3 each. Packaging is $2 per order.","Predict the cost of six badges.","Give the expected result for quantity 0.","Explain which test could reveal a packaging error."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Collect individual responses: quantity 6 gives $20 and quantity 0 gives $2. A zero test exposes the packaging-per-item fault because it gives $0. Look for a requirement-based explanation and an independent expected result. Use errors to choose a brief trace or fixed-charge starter next lesson.","strategy":"Independent exit ticket"}]},{"id":"u1-w01-l04","lesson":4,"title":"Build a book-cover calculator","prepare":"Python editor, a new cover_cost.py file and a test-record template. Keep the badge example available as a reference.","slides":[{"title":"Build a book-cover calculator","body":["Unit 1 · Week 1 · Lesson 4","Plan, implement, test and explain a new problem."],"minutes":2,"notes":"This is formative transfer practice, not an additional summative assessment. Let learners use their earlier notes while making their own design and test decisions.","strategy":"Learning intention and transfer"},{"title":"Retrieve a useful test habit","body":["Write why we predict before running.","Explain a fixed charge using the badge example.","Compare explanations with a partner.","Add one detail that makes your answer clearer."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Allow one minute of writing, two minutes comparing, then two minutes sharing corrections. Expected: an independent prediction checks correctness, a fixed charge applies once per order and a trace finds the first wrong value. Invite learners to use a small numerical example if language is a barrier.","strategy":"Retrieval with peer explanation"},{"title":"The book-cover requirement","body":["Each book cover costs $2.","Every order has a $4 handling charge.","The handling charge also applies to a zero-cover order.","Assume the quantity is a whole number of zero or more."],"minutes":6,"notes":"Learners underline the input, circle the two prices and mark the zero-order rule in their own notes. Ask everyone to predict whether quantity 0 costs $0 or $4 and explain. Correct $4 under this requirement. Success means an IPO plan, readable saved script, three predicted tests and an explanation of a line. Validation comes later.","strategy":"Requirement annotation"},{"title":"Design before coding","body":["Draft your own input, processing and output plan.","Write the calculation in words.","Ask a partner to follow the steps for two covers.","Revise your plan before coding."],"minutes":10,"notes":"Five minutes independent design, three minutes partner walkthrough, two minutes revision. Expected quantity × 2 + 4, so two covers cost $8. Partners ask questions without writing the solution for each other. Support: offer a blank IPO frame. Stretch: explain how a change to handling would affect every order. Suggested names COVER_PRICE and HANDLING.","strategy":"Independent design and peer critique"},{"title":"Expected results come from the rule","table":[["Covers","Your working","Expected total"],["0","Calculate on paper","Record prediction"],["1","Calculate on paper","Record prediction"],["5","Calculate on paper","Record prediction"]],"minutes":10,"notes":"Learners calculate all three cases individually before any program runs. Expected 0 × 2 + 4 = $4, 1 × 2 + 4 = $6 and 5 × 2 + 4 = $14. Ask for working, then compare in pairs. Keep answers in teacher notes so the slide does not give away the task. Support: model a different quantity, 2 gives $8. Stretch: find a test that distinguishes adding handling once from adding it per cover.","strategy":"Independent prediction with reduced support"},{"title":"Your own working calculator","body":["Create and save cover_cost.py.","Use your plan to write the calculation.","Display a labelled result and run your tests.","Record actual results beside your predictions."],"minutes":12,"notes":"Allow independent work, with short teacher conferences. Offer the badge example or a partial file only to learners who need it, then ask them to explain the changed lines. Reference: COVER_PRICE = 2; HANDLING = 4; quantity = int(input('Covers: ')); total = quantity * COVER_PRICE + HANDLING; print(f'Total: ${total}'). Stretch: change one fixed value, design a fresh test and justify it. Do not introduce input validation in this task.","strategy":"Independent practice with optional scaffolds"},{"title":"Feedback that leads to a revision","body":["Show a partner one prediction and actual result.","Explain why handling is added once.","Partner: ask one question about the evidence.","Revise one detail and name a limitation."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Each learner explains while the partner checks against the requirement. Expected test results $4, $6, $14. Feedback should identify a specific match or mismatch, not just say good work. Learners revise their own code or explanation. A suitable limitation is that invalid text and negative quantities are not handled. Keep the original expectation if a run fails.","strategy":"Peer feedback and self-explanation"},{"title":"Exit check: a fresh quantity","body":["Predict three covers without running the code.","Explain one input, processing or output line.","Which check helped you find or avoid an error?","Save your script and test evidence."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Collect individual responses before showing the answer: $10. Ask for a specific check such as predicting zero, tracing a total or comparing an actual result. Use responses to identify learners needing setup, arithmetic or tracing support before next week’s types and operators. The task remains formative.","strategy":"Independent exit ticket and reflection"}]},{"id":"u1-w02-l01","lesson":1,"title":"Values, types and input","chapter":"values","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#values","prepare":"Approved Python 3 editor, paper for type sorting and a saved Week 1 badge script.","slides":[{"title":"Values, types and input","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Explain that learners will choose a type by meaning, convert suitable input and justify a prediction. This is formative practice.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 2 · Lesson 1","Choose a type that fits what a value means."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: the badge program","strategy":"Individual retrieval and pair comparison","notes":"Allow one minute without notes, then compare with a partner. Expected quantity is input, quantity × 3 is processing, print displays output. input returns text and int converts suitable text. If learners think input returns a number, demonstrate type(input(...)) with 4 before continuing.","body":["Without notes, name the badge program’s input.","Which line calculates the cost?","Why did we use int(input(...))?","Compare with a partner, then correct your explanation."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Four useful Python types","strategy":"Type sorting and explanation","notes":"Model the first row, then learners suggest another example for each type. A count is int, a measurement can be float, an identifier stays str and a two-state value is bool. Capitalisation matters in True and False. Avoid presenting float as an exact decimal representation.","table":[["Type","Example","Meaning"],["int","24","Count of badges"],["float","2.5","Length in metres"],["str","\"0042\"","Identifier"],["bool","True","Two-state value"]],"minutes":6},{"title":"The same digits can mean different things","strategy":"Teacher think-aloud and guided prediction","notes":"Think aloud: the first line joins strings and the second adds integers. Expected 123 then 15. Learners predict first, run, then annotate which values change type. input always returns str here. int(\"12.5\") raises ValueError while float(\"12.5\") gives 12.5. Demonstrate the failure in a separate interpreter, explaining that error handling comes later.","body":["Predict the first two outputs before running.","Explain why they differ."],"code":"print(\"12\" + \"3\")\nprint(int(\"12\") + int(\"3\"))\nmeasurement = float(\"12.5\")","minutes":10},{"title":"A type depends on its purpose","strategy":"Think, pair, share","notes":"Two minutes individually choosing types, three comparing, then share justifications. Expected locker label str, student count int, temperature float and consent flag bool. A number-looking identifier does not require arithmetic. Keep a label such as 0042 as a string to preserve its zeros. Support: supply the four type names. Stretch: explain why converting an identifier may lose information.","body":["Choose a type for each value and explain why.","Locker label \"0042\" and student count 28.","Temperature 18.5 and permission True.","Compare with a partner before changing your answers."],"minutes":10},{"title":"A measurement calculator","strategy":"Predict, run, investigate","notes":"Learners predict for length 2.5 and width 4: Area: 10.0, then run using suitable numeric input. Driver runs and navigator explains conversions, then swap for 3 and 2: 6.0. Each learner records predictions and results. Support: identify which lines read, calculate and display. Stretch: explain why int(input(...)) would reject the text 2.5.","body":["Predict for length 2.5 and width 4.","Run, compare and explain one conversion."],"code":"length = float(input(\"Length: \"))\nwidth = float(input(\"Width: \"))\narea = length * width\nprint(f\"Area: {area}\")","minutes":12},{"title":"Which conversion fits?","strategy":"All-student misconception check","notes":"Collect A/B/C responses simultaneously. Correct B preserves 0042. A produces 42 and C produces 42.0. If several choose A, revisit identifier versus quantity and recheck a phone extension with a leading zero. Do not imply bool(\"False\") parses a Boolean word; non-empty strings are truthy, which is beyond today’s input task.","body":["An identifier must keep its leading zeros.","A: int(\"0042\")     B: \"0042\"     C: float(\"0042\")","Choose alone, then justify your answer.","What information could conversion remove?"],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: a type and a reason","strategy":"Independent exit ticket","notes":"Expected str for 0075, float for 1.75 metres, int for 6 items and bool for an on/off flag. \"6\" + \"2\" gives \"62\" and int(\"6\") + 2 gives 8. Collect without partner help. Use errors to group a brief type/conversion refresher next lesson.","body":["Choose types for label \"0075\" and length 1.75.","Choose types for a count and an on/off flag.","Predict \"6\" + \"2\" and int(\"6\") + 2.","Give a reason for one choice."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w02-l02","lesson":2,"title":"Operators and changing values","chapter":"values","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#values","prepare":"Python editor, paper trace tables and counters or a sketch of objects in groups.","slides":[{"title":"Operators and changing values","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Link to Week 1 calculations. Success means predict arithmetic, explain precedence, trace an update and interpret quotient/remainder for non-negative quantities.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 2 · Lesson 2","Predict calculations before you run them."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: text or number?","strategy":"Individual retrieval and peer explanation","notes":"Expected \"45\", 9 and float for a fractional measurement. Give one minute individually, then compare explanations. If string concatenation still causes confusion, show the two expressions side by side before introducing additional operators.","body":["Predict \"4\" + \"5\" without running it.","Predict int(\"4\") + 5.","Which type suits a measurement with a fraction?","Explain one answer to a partner."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Arithmetic operators","strategy":"Worked examples","notes":"Model the first four operators briefly, then use counters for 17 objects packed four per group. / returns a float for these operands, so 17 / 4 is 4.25. // floors the quotient, giving 4, and % gives remainder 1. Restrict grouping tasks to non-negative integers and positive group sizes. A zero divisor raises ZeroDivisionError.","table":[["Operator","Example","Result"],["+ and -","7 + 2, 7 - 2","9, 5"],["* and /","3 * 4, 17 / 4","12, 4.25"],["//","17 // 4","4 full groups"],["%","17 % 4","1 remaining"]],"minutes":6},{"title":"Order changes the result","strategy":"Teacher think-aloud and guided practice","notes":"Model multiplication before addition for 2 + 3 * 4 = 14. Parentheses first gives (2 + 3) * 4 = 20. Learners then independently predict 18 - 6 / 3 = 16.0. Multiplication and division share precedence and evaluate left to right here. Check all responses before moving on. Support: underline the first operation.","body":["2 + 3 * 4 gives 14.","(2 + 3) * 4 gives 20.","Predict 18 - 6 / 3 and show your working.","Compare the first operation with a partner."],"minutes":10},{"title":"Assignment updates the stored value","strategy":"Worked trace and faded support","notes":"Model count = 4, then the right side count + 1 uses the old 4 and assigns 5, then multiplying by 2 assigns 10. Learners annotate the value after each line and predict print output 10. Repeat independently from count 7, giving 7, 8, 16. = assigns and == compares. Python assignment is not a mathematical claim that count equals count plus one.","body":["Write the value of count after each assignment.","Try again with a starting value of 7."],"code":"count = 4\ncount = count + 1\ncount = count * 2\nprint(count)","minutes":10},{"title":"Cones packed in boxes","strategy":"Paired coding with role changes","notes":"For 29 cones and six per box, expected full boxes 4 and remaining 5. Learners complete and run the code, then swap driver/navigator roles for tests 0, 6 and 7. Expected (0,0), (1,0), (1,1). Each learner keeps a test record. Support: give the operator meanings. Stretch: verify full_boxes * BOX_SIZE + remaining equals cones.","body":["Replace each __ with the correct operator.","Predict and test for 0, 6 and 7 cones."],"code":"cones = 29\nBOX_SIZE = 6\nfull_boxes = cones __ BOX_SIZE\nremaining = cones __ BOX_SIZE\nprint(full_boxes, remaining)","minutes":12},{"title":"Division gives different information","strategy":"Hinge question and error analysis","notes":"Correct B is 2 full boxes. A gives 2.3333333333333335 and C gives remainder 2. Ask for simultaneous responses, then an explanation. If learners confuse quotient and remainder, draw two groups of six and circle the two leftovers. Recheck with 13 cones: quotient 2, remainder 1.","body":["Fourteen cones go into boxes of six.","Which expression gives the number of full boxes?","A: 14 / 6     B: 14 // 6     C: 14 % 6","Explain what each result tells you."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: predict and justify","strategy":"Independent exit ticket","notes":"Expected 3 + 2 * 5 = 13, (3 + 2) * 5 = 25, 23 // 5 = 4 and 23 % 5 = 3. count = 8 then count = count + 1 gives 9. Collect working rather than output copied from Python. Use errors to revisit precedence or grouping.","body":["Predict 3 + 2 * 5 and (3 + 2) * 5.","Find 23 // 5 and 23 % 5.","Trace count = 8, then count = count + 1.","Explain one answer using the operation’s meaning."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w02-l03","lesson":3,"title":"Binary, decimal and hexadecimal","chapter":"representation","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#representation","prepare":"Paper place-value grids, Python editor for checking after hand calculations.","slides":[{"title":"Binary and hexadecimal","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Explain that the base changes notation while the value stays the same. Success means hand conversions of small non-negative integers and a second-method check.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 2 · Lesson 3","Use place values to explain the same number in different bases."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: decimal place value","strategy":"Think, pair, share","notes":"Expected 247 = 2 × 100 + 4 × 10 + 7 × 1. The left position is ten times the next position to the right. Let learners explain with a place-value grid. This bridge prepares doubling for binary without assuming prior binary knowledge.","body":["Write 247 as hundreds, tens and ones.","Explain what the 4 contributes.","How does a position change as you move left?","Compare your explanation with a partner."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Binary place values","strategy":"Worked example and guided practice","notes":"Binary uses only 0 and 1, with each position twice the value to its right. Model 10110₂ by summing 16 + 4 + 2 = 22 decimal. Read the table from left to right. Then learners use the same headings for 11001₂: 16 + 8 + 1 = 25. Support: circle positions with 1. Ask all learners for the decimal result.","table":[["Place","16","8","4","2","1"],["Digit","1","0","1","1","0"],["Contribution","16","0","4","2","0"]],"minutes":6},{"title":"Decimal 22 written in binary","strategy":"Teacher think-aloud and faded support","notes":"Model choosing 16 from 22, leaving 6, skipping 8, choosing 4 then 2, leaving zero. Result 10110₂. Learners convert decimal 13 independently: 01101₂ or 1101₂. Leading zeros preserve value. Support: provide place headings 16,8,4,2,1. Stretch: convert 31 and explain why adding 1 needs a new position.","body":["Choose place values that add to 22: 16 + 4 + 2.","Write 1 for chosen places and 0 for the others.","22 decimal becomes 10110 in binary.","Now convert decimal 13 and check by adding."],"minutes":10},{"title":"Hexadecimal uses sixteen digits","strategy":"Worked example and paired explanation","notes":"Explain digits 0–9 then A=10 through F=15. For 2F hexadecimal, 2 × 16 + 15 = 47 decimal. Each hex digit maps to four bits: 2 is 0010 and F is 1111, giving 00101111. Ask pairs to explain why F is one digit rather than a variable. A bit is a binary digit, and eight bits form a byte.","table":[["Hex digit","Decimal value","Four binary digits"],["2","2","0010"],["A","10","1010"],["F","15","1111"]],"minutes":10},{"title":"Conversion practice before Python","strategy":"Independent practice and peer checking","notes":"Allow seven minutes individually, three comparing working, then two using Python. Expected 11001₂ = 25; 3C₁₆ = 60 and 00111100₂; decimal 37 = 100101₂ and 25₁₆. Python bin(37), hex(37), int(\"100101\",2), int(\"25\",16) verify. Support: supply place grids and A–F values. Stretch: compare two methods for converting 3C. Do not accept Python output alone as hand-working evidence.","body":["Convert binary 11001 to decimal.","Convert hexadecimal 3C to decimal and eight-bit binary.","Convert decimal 37 to binary and hexadecimal.","Show working, compare, then check with Python."],"minutes":12},{"title":"Leading zeros and number bases","strategy":"All-student misconception check","notes":"Correct B: both equal decimal 5. Leading zeros change written width, not value. A common misconception is treating the digits 101 as decimal one hundred and one. If several choose A, return to place values 4,2,1, then recheck binary 0011 versus 11. Always state the base when notation could be ambiguous.","body":["Compare binary 00101 and binary 101.","A: different values     B: the same value","Choose alone and justify with place values.","How many bits form one byte?"],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: one value, three notations","strategy":"Independent exit ticket","notes":"Expected decimal 26 is binary 11010 and hexadecimal 1A. Working: 16 + 8 + 2 and 1 × 16 + 10. A–F are hexadecimal digits for 10–15. Collect individual work. Use errors to decide whether to revisit binary positions or hexadecimal digit values in Lesson 4.","body":["Write decimal 26 in binary and hexadecimal.","Show a calculation that checks each answer.","What does A mean as a hexadecimal digit?","Explain why the value stays the same."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w02-l04","lesson":4,"title":"Characters, code points and bytes","chapter":"representation","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#representation","prepare":"Python 3 editor capable of UTF-8, paper matching cards and Week 2 conversion notes.","slides":[{"title":"Characters, code points and bytes","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success means distinguish a character, a Unicode code point and encoded bytes. Learners revisit base conversion while investigating text representation. Keep examples to A and é to avoid implying all visible characters use exactly one code point.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 2 · Lesson 4","Explain how a character connects to a number and encoded bytes."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: the number 65","strategy":"Individual retrieval and pair explanation","notes":"Expected decimal 65 is binary 1000001 or 01000001 as eight bits, and hexadecimal 41. Working 64 + 1 and 4 × 16 + 1. Allow silent working before pairs compare. If needed, model the 64 position. Use this same value for the character A next.","body":["Write decimal 65 in binary and hexadecimal.","Show place-value working for one conversion.","Compare with a partner.","What changes when we write a number in another base?"],"minutes":5},{"title":"A character and its code point","strategy":"Worked example and matching","notes":"Explain Unicode assigns A code point 65, written U+0041 in hexadecimal. These are representations of the code-point number. UTF-8 encodes A as the single byte 41 hexadecimal. Learners match the rows to character, number and bytes. A code point identifies a character in this example; some visible characters combine multiple code points.","table":[["Representation","Example for A"],["Character","A"],["Code point","65 decimal = U+0041"],["Code-point number in binary","1000001"],["UTF-8 encoded byte","41 hexadecimal"]],"minutes":6},{"title":"Python can inspect a code point","strategy":"Predict, run, investigate","notes":"Predict 65, 0b1000001, 0x41 and A, then run. ord returns a single character’s code point, chr maps a code point back to a character. bin and hex return strings with notation prefixes. Ask learners to annotate each output as number, notation or character. This code does not show encoded bytes yet.","body":["Predict each line of output before running.","Which line returns the character?"],"code":"point = ord(\"A\")\nprint(point)\nprint(bin(point))\nprint(hex(point))\nprint(chr(point))","minutes":10},{"title":"An encoding determines the bytes","strategy":"Teacher modelling and contrast","notes":"Show A: ord 65, UTF-8 hex 41, byte length 1. Show é: ord 233 (U+00E9), UTF-8 hex c3a9, byte length 2. Run the snippet with A, then change to the single precomposed é character shown. len(encoded) counts bytes. .hex() displays those bytes in hex. Explain that a code-point number is different from the encoded byte sequence. Do not generalise one visible character to one byte.","body":["Predict whether é uses the same byte count as A.","Run and compare the two examples."],"code":"text = \"é\"\nencoded = text.encode(\"utf-8\")\nprint(ord(text))\nprint(encoded.hex())\nprint(len(encoded))","minutes":10},{"title":"A representation record","strategy":"Paired investigation with role changes","notes":"Learners complete A then swap roles for precomposed é. Expected A: code point 65/U+0041, UTF-8 41, one byte. é: 233/U+00E9, UTF-8 c3a9, two bytes. Navigator explains which column comes from ord and which from encode. Each learner records an explanation. Support: use the previous snippet. Stretch: explain why decimal 233 alone does not identify a file’s byte sequence without an encoding.","table":[["Character","Code point","UTF-8 hex","Byte count"],["A","Predict, check","Predict, check","Predict, check"],["é","Predict, check","Predict, check","Predict, check"]],"minutes":12},{"title":"How many bytes does a character use?","strategy":"Hinge question and peer explanation","notes":"Correct B. é has code point 233 but uses two UTF-8 bytes c3 a9. A uses one. Collect simultaneous responses, then ask pairs to explain using the record. If several choose A, point to the code-point and encoded-byte columns and re-run len(encoded). A byte is eight bits, but an encoded character can occupy several bytes.","body":["“Every Unicode character takes one byte in UTF-8.”","A: agree     B: disagree","Choose alone, then use A and é as evidence.","What information does a code point identify?"],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: explain the distinction","strategy":"Independent exit ticket and reflection","notes":"Expected A has decimal code point 65 and U+0041, encoded as one UTF-8 byte 41. é uses two UTF-8 bytes. Unicode provides code points and UTF-8 supplies encoding rules. Learners explain in their own words and identify a check that helped. Use remaining base-conversion or encoding confusion for a short retrieval starter before Week 3 selection.","body":["Explain character, code point and encoded bytes.","Give A’s code point in decimal and hexadecimal.","Use é to challenge the one-byte claim.","Which prediction or check changed your understanding?"],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w03-l01","lesson":1,"title":"Comparisons and two-way decisions","chapter":"selection","prepare":"Python editor, paper trace grids and the selection chapter.","slides":[{"title":"Comparisons and two-way decisions","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success: predict a Boolean comparison, trace an if/else branch and justify a boundary test. Connect selection to Week 1 sequence. This lesson assumes suitable non-negative numeric input.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 3 · Lesson 1","Use a condition to choose which instructions run."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: assignment and types","strategy":"Individual retrieval and pair comparison","notes":"One minute without notes, then compare. Expected count becomes 5 and input returns str. = assigns while == compares. Ask everyone to identify which symbol tests equality. If confusion remains, show count = 4 followed by count == 4, which evaluates True without updating count.","body":["Trace count = 4, then count = count + 1.","What type does input() return?","What does = do in Python?","Compare your answers before opening your notes."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Comparisons produce Boolean results","strategy":"Worked examples and guided prediction","notes":"Model the first two rows, then learners explain why 50 >= 50 is True while 50 > 50 is False. Cover the result column or use a fresh example: 12 \u003c= 12 is True. = assigns, == compares. True and False have capital initial letters in Python.","table":[["Comparison","Meaning","Example result"],["== and !=","Equal / not equal","5 == 5 is True"],["\u003c and >","Less / greater than","5 > 8 is False"],["\u003c=","Less than or equal","5 \u003c= 5 is True"],[">=","Greater than or equal","50 >= 50 is True"]],"minutes":6},{"title":"A delivery rule with a boundary","strategy":"Think, pair, share","notes":"Read the rule together, then allow two minutes of silent predictions. Expected delivery charges: 35 gives $5, 50 gives $0, 80 gives $0. Invite pairs to explain why the boundary belongs to the free-delivery branch. Assume order total is a valid number of zero or more. This is a teaching example, not a robust payment application.","body":["Orders of $50 or more have free delivery.","Smaller orders have a $5 delivery charge.","Predict the charge for totals 35, 50 and 80.","Explain which comparison matches “$50 or more”."],"minutes":10},{"title":"Two branches, one chosen path","strategy":"Teacher think-aloud and code annotation","notes":"Trace order_total 35: condition False, else assigns 5, final print runs and displays Delivery: $5.00. Repeat for 50: True, if assigns 0, output Delivery: $0.00. Colon introduces a suite and indentation groups its instructions. Ask learners to mark the two branches and circle the final print outside them. Invalid input handling comes later.","body":["Trace once for 35, then for 50.","Which line runs after either branch?"],"code":"order_total = float(input(\"Order total: \"))\nif order_total >= 50:\n    delivery = 0\nelse:\n    delivery = 5\nprint(f\"Delivery: ${delivery:.2f}\")","minutes":10},{"title":"A trace before each run","strategy":"Paired prediction, run and explanation","notes":"Driver enters an input while navigator checks the prior prediction and explains the selected branch. Swap after the first run. Expected 49.99 False/else/$5.00; 50 True/if/$0.00; 50.01 True/if/$0.00. Every learner records their own trace. Support: supply condition and branch headings. Stretch: explain which test detects > instead of >=.","table":[["Order total","Condition","Branch and charge"],["49.99","Predict first","Trace, then run"],["50.00","Predict first","Trace, then run"],["50.01","Predict first","Trace, then run"]],"minutes":12},{"title":"Which condition includes the boundary?","strategy":"All-student hinge question","notes":"Take simultaneous responses. Correct B is >= 50. A excludes exactly 50 and C excludes all larger orders. If several choose A, mark 50 on a number line and retest with exactly 50 before moving on. Ask a learner choosing B to explain the equality part.","body":["Which condition matches “at least 50”?","A: total > 50     B: total >= 50     C: total == 50","Choose alone and show your answer together.","Name an input that exposes each wrong choice."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: a new decision","strategy":"Independent transfer check","notes":"Museum rule: under 16 costs $8, otherwise $12. Expected age 15 gives $8, 16 gives $12, 17 gives $12. Correct condition age \u003c 16 for the cheaper branch. Collect the condition and one trace, without partner help. Revisit equality or branch tracing next lesson if needed.","body":["A museum charges $8 for ages under 16.","Everyone else pays $12.","Write the condition for the cheaper branch.","Predict prices at ages 15, 16 and 17."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w03-l02","lesson":2,"title":"Combining Boolean conditions","chapter":"selection","prepare":"Python editor, paper response cards and a four-row truth-table frame.","slides":[{"title":"Combining Boolean conditions","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success: explain and/or/not, evaluate a combined rule and use parentheses to make grouping explicit. Use supplied Boolean values rather than parsing yes/no input.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 3 · Lesson 2","Explain when a combined condition becomes True."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: the equality boundary","strategy":"Individual retrieval with correction","notes":"Expected False, True and True. Ask for one minute of individual work, then paired explanations. If > and >= remain confused, return to the number-line boundary before combining conditions.","body":["Predict 50 > 50 and 50 >= 50.","Predict 15 \u003c 16.","Explain one answer to a partner.","Correct any disagreement using the comparison’s meaning."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Both, either and the opposite","strategy":"Worked truth table","notes":"Model the first row and ask learners to predict the remaining rows before revealing them or checking with Python. and requires both True, or needs at least one True and includes both, not reverses a Boolean. Here P and Q stand for Boolean values. A common error is interpreting or as exactly one.","table":[["P","Q","P and Q","P or Q"],["True","True","True","True"],["True","False","False","True"],["False","True","False","True"],["False","False","False","False"]],"minutes":6},{"title":"An eligibility rule","strategy":"Teacher modelling and paired variation","notes":"Rule: eligibility requires age at least 16 AND permission. With supplied values age 16 and has_permission False, result is False. Ask learners to predict changing permission to True, then age to 15. Expected True then False. has_permission is already bool. bool(\"False\") is True because the string is non-empty, so do not use it to parse input.","body":["Predict before running.","Change one value, then explain the new result."],"code":"age = 16\nhas_permission = False\neligible = age >= 16 and has_permission\nprint(eligible)","minutes":10},{"title":"Grouping changes which rule you test","strategy":"Guided prediction and peer explanation","notes":"Use the stated values to evaluate both expressions. A is True because and binds more tightly than or. B is False because the parenthesised or is True but not suspended is False. Explain Python Boolean operator precedence: not, then and, then or. Comparisons here evaluate before these Boolean combinations. Ask learners to annotate sub-results. If confusion remains, replace each sub-expression with True/False on paper.","body":["member = True, guest = False, suspended = True","A: member or guest and not suspended","B: (member or guest) and not suspended","Predict each result and explain the grouping."],"minutes":10},{"title":"Testing every Boolean combination","strategy":"Paired investigation with role changes","notes":"Use supplied booleans rather than text input. Learners implement allowed = (member or guest) and not suspended and choose cases before running. Expected True/False/False gives True; False/True/False gives True; True/True/True gives False; False/False/False gives False. These cover key paths but not all eight combinations. Stretch: test all eight and justify coverage. Support: add intermediate (member or guest) results.","body":["Rule: a member or guest may enter if not suspended.","Write the condition with explicit parentheses.","Choose cases for each route to entry and refusal.","Predict, run and compare, then swap roles."],"minutes":12},{"title":"What does or allow?","strategy":"All-student misconception check","notes":"Collect responses before discussion. Correct B: True or True is True. Explain inclusive or allows both. If learners choose A, use the truth-table first row and recheck True or False. Ask them to contrast and, which requires both.","body":["Predict True or True.","A: False     B: True","Show your choice, then explain it.","How would and differ when only one value is True?"],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: a combined decision","strategy":"Independent exit ticket","notes":"Rule: both age >= 16 and has_permission, reversed permission with not. Expected age 16/permission False gives False for eligibility, age 15/permission True gives False and age 16/permission True gives True. not False is True. Collect without Python first. Group follow-up support by comparison, conjunction or negation errors.","body":["Rule: age >= 16 and has_permission.","Predict for (16, False), (15, True), (16, True).","Predict not False.","Explain why one unsuccessful case fails."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w03-l03","lesson":3,"title":"Several choices with if and elif","chapter":"selection","prepare":"Python editor and paper trace tables. Use the chapter’s supplied score-band rule.","slides":[{"title":"Several choices with if and elif","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success: implement a three-way decision, trace the first matching branch and explain why order matters. This lesson uses the supplied score-band example from the chapter.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 3 · Lesson 3","Choose the first matching branch in an ordered decision."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: two-way selection","strategy":"Individual retrieval and peer explanation","notes":"Ask learners to sketch if/else structure and mark indentation. Expected one of the two branches runs and a print after the decision runs either way. == compares while = assigns. Revisit structural errors before adding elif.","body":["Sketch an if/else decision from memory.","What determines which branch runs?","Where could you print a result after either branch?","Compare the indentation with a partner."],"minutes":5},{"title":"A supplied score-band rule","strategy":"Requirement annotation","notes":"Assume a suitable whole-number score from 0 to 100. Model 85 as High. Ask learners independently for 65 Pass and 40 Developing. Have them underline the inclusive boundary at 80 and 50. These are example program categories, not official ATAR grade boundaries.","table":[["Score","Band"],["80 to 100","High"],["50 to 79","Pass"],["0 to 49","Developing"]],"minutes":6},{"title":"An ordered decision in Python","strategy":"Teacher think-aloud and guided trace","notes":"For 80 the first comparison is True, so High runs and the remaining branches are skipped. For 65 the first is False, the elif is True and Pass runs. For 40 both comparisons are False and else runs. elif belongs to the same chain. Colon and indentation matter. Learners annotate the checked conditions, not only the output.","body":["Trace scores 80, 65 and 40."],"code":"score = 80\nif score >= 80:\n    band = \"High\"\nelif score >= 50:\n    band = \"Pass\"\nelse:\n    band = \"Developing\"\nprint(band)","minutes":10},{"title":"Why the order matters","strategy":"Hinge question and error analysis","notes":"Take predictions before running. With 85, the first >= 50 comparison is True, so band becomes Pass and the elif is skipped. This is a logic error for the stated rule. Learners reorder the >= 80 check before >= 50 and test 85 again. If several still expect High automatically, trace line by line and cross out skipped branches.","body":["Which band does this code assign?","Explain the fault, then correct the branch order."],"code":"score = 85\nif score >= 50:\n    band = \"Pass\"\nelif score >= 80:\n    band = \"High\"\nelse:\n    band = \"Developing\"","minutes":10},{"title":"Your own implementation and test record","strategy":"Independent coding with optional scaffolds","notes":"Learners implement the correct ordered rule and record predictions before runs. Expected 49 Developing, 50 Pass, 79 Pass, 80 High. Support: provide a skeleton with blank conditions, then ask learners to explain their choices. Stretch: compare an ordered chain with two independent if statements and investigate whether later assignments overwrite a result. Keep the supplied 0–100 valid-input assumption.","body":["Implement the supplied score-band rule.","Predict bands for 49, 50, 79 and 80.","Run each case and retain your expected results.","Explain any correction using a trace."],"minutes":12},{"title":"One chain or separate decisions?","strategy":"Paired prediction and whole-class check","notes":"Trace this deliberate fault at score 85: first if assigns High; second independent if is also True and overwrites band with Pass. Output Pass. Contrast with the correct if/elif chain, which prints High. If learners expect High, ask which assignment runs last. This is about separate if statements, not a claim that all if statements should use elif.","body":["Predict the output before discussing it.","How does a second if differ from elif?"],"code":"score = 85\nif score >= 80:\n    band = \"High\"\nif score >= 50:\n    band = \"Pass\"\nprint(band)","minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: branch and reason","strategy":"Independent exit ticket","notes":"Expected 79 Pass, 80 High and 50 Pass. 80 belongs to High because the >= 80 branch is checked first and includes equality. Use responses to select a short first-match tracing starter for Lesson 4.","body":["State the bands for scores 79, 80 and 50.","Which condition matches first for score 80?","Explain why checking >= 50 first causes a fault.","Save your code and test evidence."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w03-l04","lesson":4,"title":"Boundary tests and debugging","chapter":"selection","prepare":"Python editor, a clean delivery example and a blank expected/actual test log.","slides":[{"title":"Boundary tests and debugging","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success: derive tests from a requirement, identify a boundary fault, correct it and rerun the relevant tests. Expected results must come from the stated rule rather than the faulty code.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 3 · Lesson 4","Use expected results to find and explain a decision fault."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: where do results change?","strategy":"Individual retrieval and pair comparison","notes":"Ask learners to identify the two score boundaries 50 and 80, then the delivery boundary 50. Rehearse below/at/above with valid integer scores: 49,50,51 and 79,80,81. For delivery totals to cents use 49.99,50.00,50.01. Distinguish the input domains rather than assuming every task uses the same step.","body":["Where do the score-band decisions change?","Where does the free-delivery decision change?","Choose a value just below one boundary.","Compare your reasoning with a partner."],"minutes":5},{"title":"A test record keeps expectation separate","strategy":"Worked example and guided completion","notes":"Delivery rule stays $0 for totals >= 50 and $5 otherwise. Model 49.99 with condition False and expected $5.00. Learners complete expected values for 50 and 50.01 before running. Actual and pass/fail remain blank until execution. Expected $0.00 for both. If learners copy actual into expected, revisit the requirement and preserve an independent prediction.","table":[["Input total","Expected charge","Actual / result"],["49.99","$5.00","Record after run"],["50.00","Predict from rule","Record after run"],["50.01","Predict from rule","Record after run"]],"minutes":6},{"title":"A fault that ordinary tests can miss","strategy":"Predict, run, investigate","notes":"Run this deliberately faulty example with 10 and 100: both match the stated rule. Then 50 prints Delivery: $5.00 but expectation is $0.00. The condition > excludes the boundary. Ask learners to record the mismatch before editing. Keep suitable non-negative input; invalid input handling is separate.","body":["Predict from the requirement for 10, 100 and 50.","Run each case and locate the mismatch."],"code":"order_total = float(input(\"Order total: \"))\nif order_total > 50:\n    delivery = 0\nelse:\n    delivery = 5\nprint(f\"Delivery: ${delivery:.2f}\")","minutes":10},{"title":"A correction needs an explanation","strategy":"Guided debugging and peer explanation","notes":"Learners explain that >= includes exactly 50, change the faulty comparison and rerun 49.99,50,50.01,10,100. Expected charges 5,0,0,5,0. Keep the earlier failed result alongside the corrected run. A successful test is evidence for tested cases, not proof for every possible input. Support: trace only the comparison at 50 first.","body":["Use a trace to explain the wrong charge at 50.","Correct the comparison and predict again.","Rerun below, at and above the boundary.","Retest ordinary cases and record the correction."],"minutes":10},{"title":"Transfer: museum ticket prices","strategy":"Independent design and testing","notes":"Rule age \u003c 16 costs $8, otherwise $12; valid non-negative integer ages. Learners choose 15,16,17 plus an ordinary younger/older case before coding. Model solution: age = int(input(\"Age: \")); if age \u003c 16: ticket = 8; else: ticket = 12; print(ticket), with proper indentation on separate lines. Expected 8,12,12. Support: supply an if/else skeleton. Stretch: show a \u003c= fault and identify the detecting input. Partners may question the test evidence after independent work.","body":["Under 16 costs $8. Everyone else pays $12.","Design boundary tests before writing the program.","Implement, run and record expected versus actual.","Ask a partner to challenge one test choice."],"minutes":12},{"title":"What do passing tests show?","strategy":"All-student misconception check","notes":"Correct B: passing 10 and 100 does not establish that equality is handled. The > fault passes those two cases but fails 50. Collect responses before showing the faulty code again. If learners choose A, ask them to supply the missing boundary expectation. No finite small set proves all possible behaviours.","body":["Delivery tests at 10 and 100 both pass.","“This proves the boundary is correct.”","A: agree     B: disagree","Choose, then give a test that strengthens the evidence."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: justify a test set","strategy":"Independent exit ticket and reflection","notes":"For museum ages 15,16,17 expected 8,12,12. Age 16 detects \u003c= 16 used for the cheaper branch, because that fault gives 8 instead of 12. Learners explain how they derived expectations and one correction. Collect evidence to identify comparison, branch-order or test-design support before Week 4 loops.","body":["Give museum tests just below, at and above 16.","Write the expected prices from the rule.","Which test detects age \u003c= 16 as a fault?","Explain one check you will reuse in future programs."],"minutes":5}]}]};
